1. AIJ pedestrian-wind benchmark data
-------------------------------------

Publisher:  Architectural Institute of Japan (AIJ)
Source:     https://www.aij.or.jp/jpn/publish/cfdguide/index_e.htm
Reference:  Tominaga, Mochida, Yoshie, Kataoka, Nozu, Yoshikawa & Shirasawa (2008),
            "AIJ guidelines for practical applications of CFD to pedestrian wind
            environment around buildings", J. Wind Eng. Ind. Aerodyn. 96(10-11),
            1749-1761.

The wind-tunnel measurements and geometry definitions published by AIJ for the
guideline benchmark cases are used here as validation targets. Case A measurements
are the split-film data of Meng & Hibi (1998) as distributed by AIJ.

NEITHER the AIJ workbooks NOR any values derived from them are redistributed in
this repository. What ships here is only our own code: the loaders, the scoring
implementation, and the conversion scripts in scripts/.

To validate against these cases, download the workbooks from the AIJ URL above and
run the conversion scripts locally. docs/BENCHMARK-DATA.md gives the procedure and
the SHA-256 of each workbook, so the exact inputs behind the results recorded in
docs/VALIDATION.md can be identified and reproduced.

Absent that data, apps/studio/src/sim/cases/data/aij-case-a.json holds a SYNTHETIC
placeholder generated by scripts/make-synthetic-case-a.mjs. It contains no AIJ
content, exists only so the project builds and tests on a fresh clone, and anything
scored against it is labelled SYNTHETIC and is never presented as a result.

The published geometry parameters of the benchmark configurations (for example Case
A's 1:1:2 prism with b = 0.08 m at 1/250 scale) are stated in the Tominaga et al.
paper cited above and are used here as ordinary factual citations.


2. Lid-driven cavity reference data
-----------------------------------

Reference:  Ghia, U., Ghia, K.N. & Shin, C.T. (1982), "High-Re solutions for
            incompressible flow using the Navier-Stokes equations and a multigrid
            method", J. Comput. Phys. 48, 387-411.

Tabulated centerline velocity values from that paper (Re=100 and Re=1000 columns)
are reproduced in packages/core/src/fixtures/ghia.ts as validation targets, with
provenance notes in docs/handoff/H9-fixtures-ghia.md.


3. Algorithms
-------------

All solver algorithms are implemented from published literature, not ported from
other implementations. In particular the in-place streaming scheme follows
Lehmann, M. (2022), "Esoteric Pull and Esoteric Push: Two Simple In-Place
Streaming Schemes for the Lattice Boltzmann Method on GPUs", Computation 10(6):92
(MDPI, CC BY 4.0). No code from FluidX3D or any other non-permissively licensed
solver has been read, fetched or ported — see the clean-room rule in CLAUDE.md.
